```markdown
## What an agent actually gets from a codebase

Agent prompt:
> "Where is payment validation handled, and who calls it?"

```jsonc
// call 1 — agent asks for the symbol by name
{"tool": "find_symbol", "args": {"name": "validatePayment"}}
```
→
```jsonc
{
  "matches": [
    {"file": "internal/billing/validator.go",   "line": 14, "kind": "method",   "language": "go"},
    {"file": "internal/billing/stripe.go",      "line": 42, "kind": "function", "language": "go"},
    {"file": "test/helpers/payment_test.go",    "line":  7, "kind": "function", "language": "go"}
  ]
}
```
Three hits across two source files and one test file. The agent picks the `validator.go` method.

```jsonc
// call 2 — definition lookup crosses into the interface
{"tool": "get_definitions", "args": {"symbol_ref": "validator.go:14"}}
```
→
```jsonc
{
  "definitions": [
    {"file": "internal/billing/validator.go", "line": 14, "kind": "method",   "language": "go"},
    {"file": "internal/billing/billing.go",   "line": 22, "kind": "interface","language": "go"}
  ],
  "provenance": "gopls"          // ← LSP-backed, distinct from calls 1/4/5
}
```
The method resolves to its interface declaration in a third file. This is the resolved-symbol beat — the agent didn't have to grep a second time.

```jsonc
// call 3 — type signature for the interface
{"tool": "get_symbols", "args": {"file": "internal/billing/billing.go", "kind": "interface"}}
```
→ type signature, fields, embedded types, rendered once.

```jsonc
// call 4 — who calls validatePayment in production?
{"tool": "get_callers", "args": {"symbol_ref": "validator.go:14"}}
```
→
```jsonc
{
  "callers": [
    {"file": "internal/api/checkout.go", "line": 88, "via": "CheckoutHandler.Process"},
    {"file": "cmd/server/main.go",       "line": 31, "via": "main"}
  ]
}
```
Two production callers, no test caller shown (filtered).

```jsonc
// call 5 — text snippet around the validator for human-in-the-loop confirmation
{"tool": "get_snippet", "args": {"file": "internal/billing/validator.go", "line": 14, "lines": 12}}
```
→ 12-line snippet, no surrounding test boilerplate.

Five calls. The agent now has: location, interface contract, type shape, callers, source snippet — and one of those five calls carried a `provenance: gopls` stamp so the LSP fallback is *visible*, not asserted.

**Caption under the demo:** *call 2 used gopls; calls 1, 3, 4, 5 used tree-sitter with cross-reference resolution against the parsed AST. yactt stamps every response with its provenance so the agent — and you — can tell which path served the answer.*
```

**Three notes on what this transcript does that an abstract description couldn't.**

The ambiguity is on the page. Three hits for `validatePayment` is what `find_symbol` actually returns in a real repo, and watching the agent pick one and follow it is the resolution beat in narrative form. A transcript that opened with a single-hit call would have been selling search, not resolution. The reader sees the agent *navigating*, which is the property tree-sitter-only tools can't deliver.

The provenance stamp is mid-transcript, not in a footnote. Call 2 carrying `provenance: gopls` while the other four carry tree-sitter lets the caption make a falsifiable claim: *look at call 2, the LSP path was used here*. A reader who runs yactt on a Go file with no gopls available will see all five calls stamped `tree-sitter` and the caption still holds — it's a description of *available* paths, not a promise about a specific call.

The signature rendering is concretized. `get_symbols` returning fields and embedded types for the interface is the kind of output an agent feeds back into its own reasoning. Without it, the demo is a string-matching showcase. With it, the demo is "the agent now knows the shape of the contract it's calling."

**On the receipts section — taking it from here with the inline-anchor structure you proposed.** Each strip claim gets a footnote-style anchor, the full receipts section sits between the tools table and install, titled "What's behind the badge row." The four receipts: SLSA L3 → the `provenance.intoto.jsonl` attached to the release, with the command to verify it. 21 tools → `mcp serve --list-tools` against the binary on disk. 1 dep → `go.mod` rendered in the section, `require` block visible. Read-only → the source-tree note that no tool mutates a path outside `~/.yactt/cache/` (the persisted-query store), plus a one-liner showing the registry subset is GET-only against any registered remote. Each receipt is short enough to fit between tools and install without crowding.

**One thing I'm leaving out of the demo on purpose: `get_hover` and `find_references`.** They exist, they're in the 16-tool set, but adding them pushes the transcript past "worked example" into "tour." Five calls is the right ceiling — at six, the reader's eye-glaze kicks in and the resolution beat loses its punch. The tools table covers the rest.

